<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The packaging project. It compiles nothing: its whole job is to wrap templates/ as a
    PackageType=Template NuGet package so `dotnet new install Bennewitz.Ninja.Templates` works.
    The template content itself lives under templates/ and is never built from here.

    ⭐ The id is PLURAL and generic because a template package is a CONTAINER: it bundles one or
    more templates. `bbpkg` is the first; a second ships inside this same package rather than
    needing a new id. That also matches the ecosystem — Microsoft.Android.Templates,
    Microsoft.iOS.Templates, Aspire.ProjectTemplates.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Bennewitz.Ninja.Templates</PackageId>
    <PackageType>Template</PackageType>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IncludeContentInPack>true</IncludeContentInPack>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <ContentTargetFolders>content</ContentTargetFolders>
    <PackageTags>template;dotnet-new;nuget;trusted-publishing;scaffold</PackageTags>
    <Description>
      A dotnet new template and GitHub template for Bennewitz.Ninja NuGet packages: CI, a release
      workflow with a credential preflight, the two-list packaging guard that keeps a local-only
      package off nuget.org, and the publishing runbook.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      ⛔ NU5128 is unavoidable here and Directory.Build.props turns it into an error.

      A template package ships no lib/ or ref/ assemblies by design, which is exactly what NU5128
      reports; TreatWarningsAsErrors then fails the pack. Reproduced against this family's real
      props before this line existed.

      ⚠ IncludeContentInPack=false is NOT the alternative fix. It gates the whole _GetPackageFiles
      target, which drops the README that Directory.Build.props packs, and pack dies with NU5017
      "cannot create a package that has no dependencies nor content" instead.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>

    <!--
      ⛔ NuGet excludes files starting with '.' from a package BY DEFAULT, which for a template
      means the generated repo silently arrives with no .gitignore and no .gitattributes — the
      files most likely to be missed precisely because nobody opens them.

      ⚠ Without TreatWarningsAsErrors this is NU5119, a warning, and the broken template ships. The
      family props promoting it to an error is the only reason it surfaced here rather than in
      somebody's generated repo.
    -->
    <NoDefaultExcludes>true</NoDefaultExcludes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      ⛔ PackagePath is explicit, NOT left to ContentTargetFolders.

      Relying on ContentTargetFolders alone has two failure modes, both reproduced, and the second
      is the dangerous one:

        1. A source folder named "content" packs to content/content/... and is never found.
        2. Content outside this project's cone is FLATTENED, which drops .template.config/
           entirely — and the resulting package still installs, still lists, and still "generates",
           emitting the extracted nupkg instead of a project. Every check written against an exit
           code passes on that package.

      So the recursive path is preserved by hand, and the tests assert the generated TREE.
    -->
    <!--
      ⛔ The path is the bare ROOT. NuGet appends %(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension) itself.

      Both more explicit-looking forms are wrong, and both were tried:

        content\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)
          correct for every file WITH an extension, and wrong for every file without one. NuGet
          treats a PackagePath whose last segment has no extension as a folder, so LICENSE packed
          to content/bbpkg/LICENSE/bbpkg/LICENSE. Dockerfile and Makefile would do the same.

        content\%(RecursiveDir)
          a folder, so NuGet appends the recursive path again: content/bbpkg/bbpkg/.gitignore.

      Leaving it at the root lets NuGet do the whole job, which it does correctly for extensionless
      files and dotfiles alike.

      ⛔ FORWARD SLASH, and the trailing one is load-bearing. NuGet decides "folder or file?" by
      whether PackagePath ends in a directory separator — and a backslash is only a separator on
      Windows. `content\` therefore worked here and FLATTENED on Linux, dropping
      .template.config/ from the package: the exact failure the block above exists to prevent,
      reintroduced by the separator rather than by the path.

      ⚠ It was invisible for as long as the only evidence was a Windows pack. The release runs on
      ubuntu-latest, so the first real release would have published a template that installs,
      lists and "generates" while emitting the extracted nupkg. Caught by verify-release on this
      repository's own first CI run, which is the entire argument for running it there rather
      than trusting a green local check.

      ⓘ Forward slashes work on both platforms in Include and Exclude too, so all three are
      spelled the same way and none of them depends on where the build happens to run.
    -->
    <Content Include="templates/**/*"
             Exclude="templates/**/bin/**;templates/**/obj/**"
             PackagePath="content/" />
    <Compile Remove="**/*" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
